 <section class="three-tool-section" id="gold-purity"><h3>How gold purity changes Zakat valuation</h3>
 <p>A 24-karat gold price is the price per gram of essentially pure gold. A 22k bracelet contains approximately <strong>22/24 = 91.67%</strong> fine gold by metal weight; an 18k necklace contains <strong>18/24 = 75%</strong>. Multiply gross gold-alloy mass (excluding stones) by that ratio before applying the pure-metal price.</p>
 <p>The interface supports 24k, 22k, 21k, 18k, 14k and 9k, plus separate items for different purities. It gives an <em>indicative metal-content estimate</em>, not a guaranteed resale quote. A purchase receipt may include craftsmanship, retailer margin or gemstones; those are not 24k metal. If a hallmark, stone weight or plating is unclear, have the metal assessed rather than guessing.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="gold-jewellery"><h3>Is gold jewellery included in Zakat? Scholarly differences</h3>
 <p>The <strong>Hanafi school</strong> generally includes personal-use gold and silver jewellery in Zakat calculations. The <strong>Shafi'i, Maliki and Hanbali</strong> approaches can exempt customary permissible personal-use jewellery, subject to qualifications. Gold held as investment or goods for sale may have different treatment. The precise boundaries and exceptions cannot be set by choosing a karat percentage.</p>
 <p>For a contemporary scholar-reviewed explanation, see <a href="https://www.islamic-relief.org.uk/giving/islamic-giving/zakat/zakat-on-jewellery/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Islamic Relief UK's guide to Zakat on jewellery</a>. Even if personal jewellery is excluded under your guidance, that does not necessarily exempt your savings or other qualifying wealth. Use the <a href="/tools/zakat-calculator/">full Zakat calculator</a> when reviewing all asset categories.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="gold-nisab"><h3>Gold and silver Nisab and the lunar-year condition</h3>
 <p>The current UK reference weights used here are <strong>87.48g gold</strong> and <strong>612.36g silver</strong>. Multiplying by verified per-gram prices expresses each benchmark in money. These figures are not interchangeable with kilograms of household jewellery and are not the only scholarly conversion conventions in use. <a href="https://www.islamic-relief.org.uk/giving/islamic-giving/zakat/nisab/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Islamic Relief UK's Nisab reference</a> explains the two bases.</p>
 <p><a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1405" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1405</a> reports a classical silver threshold in awaq. Modern gram equivalents derive from historical weight conversion. The selected gold or silver benchmark may produce very different eligibility results, so the tool requires you to choose a benchmark rather than silently applying the cheaper one.</p>
 <p><em>Hawl</em> refers to a lunar year relevant to most qualifying forms of wealth. The precise treatment of holdings during that year and the permitted liabilities must be assessed under qualified guidance; reaching a threshold in this calculator alone does not establish that the annual Zakat is due.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="gold-steps"><h3>Calculate Zakat on multiple gold items step by step</h3>
 <ol class="three-tool-steps"><li>Decide whether the particular jewellery or investment gold must be included under your school.</li><li>Separate out gems and non-gold parts. Enter the gold-alloy weight and purity for each item, not the full ornament or retail purchase price.</li><li>Find a currently verified 24k price per gram for your chosen currency and date. Changing currency clears monetary inputs so you cannot accidentally relabel amounts.</li><li>Add other qualifying wealth (cash or savings, for example) only if appropriate, and deduct only allowed liabilities. Do not duplicate gold already represented above.</li><li>Choose the gold or silver Nisab benchmark advised for your assessment. Silver selection requires a verified silver price.</li><li>Read the 2.5% calculation as conditional, check hawl and your obligations externally, then export a local summary if needed.</li></ol></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="gold-example"><h3>Worked example: mixed 22k and 18k gold</h3>
 <div class="three-tool-example"><p><strong>Hypothetical prices:</strong> A 30g 22k piece contains <strong>27.5g</strong> fine gold and a 20g 18k piece contains <strong>15g</strong>. Combined pure-gold equivalent is <strong>42.5g</strong>. At an invented 24k price of <strong>£80 per gram</strong>, the combined indicative metal value is <strong>£3,400</strong>.</p>
 <p>Add £1,000 of independently confirmed other qualifying assets and £100 in permitted deductions: <strong>£3,400 + £1,000 − £100 = £4,300</strong>. At an illustrative silver rate of £1.20/g, silver Nisab is <strong>£734.83</strong>, so that benchmark is exceeded. The conditional 2.5% on the combined £4,300 is <strong>£107.50</strong>. At the gold benchmark (£6,998.40), the same £4,300 would be below threshold. Those are <em>not current metal prices or instructions to choose either rule</em>.</p></div>
 <p>The calculator also shows the gold portion separately, but the overall obligation is considered against <em>combined qualifying net wealth</em>, not a separate 2.5% charge for every item regardless of eligibility. Always confirm which assets, jewellery and deductions apply.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="gold-differences"><h3>What is included in gold Zakat versus excluded from the metal estimate?</h3>
 <div class="three-tool-comparison"><div><h4>Fine gold</h4><p>Metal content at the chosen pure-gold market price, adjusted by karat.</p></div><div><h4>Gems and stones</h4><p>Not part of the pure-gold mass calculation, though items held for trade may need separate assessment.</p></div><div><h4>Retail work and premiums</h4><p>Not automatically equal to metal value; the calculator is not a jeweller's resale valuation.</p></div><div><h4>Other assets</h4><p>Savings, cash and other holdings can matter to total Zakat. Use the wider wealth calculation.</p></div></div></section>
